<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bhavnagar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1723 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Bhavnagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Gohil Rajput port state of eastern Kathiawar whose last ruler was the first prince to hand his state over to the Indian people.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohil">Gohil Rajputs</a> had ruled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> since the 13th century from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sihor">Sihor</a>. In 1723 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavsinhji-i">Bhavsinhji I</a> founded the port of Bhavnagar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a> and moved his capital there, giving the state its name. It was also known as Gohilwad after the ruling clan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bhavnagar grew rich from sea trade, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, and cooperated with the British against piracy in the Gulf. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takhtsinhji">Takhtsinhji</a> it built railways, colleges and hospitals and was regarded as one of the better-administered states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency">Kathiawar Agency</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhashankar-pattani">Prabhashankar Pattani</a> as a noted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In January 1948 Krishnakumarsinhji announced the transfer of power to a popular government, and Bhavnagar was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948; it has been in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> since 1960. Krishnakumarsinhji later served as Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bhavnagar-state.jpg" alt="Bhavnagar State" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Andreas Birken at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaurashtraKart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1723 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bhavnagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhavsinhji I, Takhtsinhji, Krishnakumarsinhji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bhavnagar, Gohilwad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=bhavnagar-state" aria-label="Bhavnagar State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6616 1373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bhavnagar State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6936 1629L7364 1601M6936 1629L7364 1602M6936 1629L6896 1610"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6896 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6936 1629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6936 1629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bhavnagar. <a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=bhavnagar-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
